  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 236 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers are tracking across the southern\nAdirondacks into south-central Vermont this hour and will\ncontinue to move through over the morning hours. While a rumble\nof thunder cannot be completely ruled out. Another area of\ninstability is moving through southern Canada towards the \nborder. Isolated to scattered showers are possible in this area \nthrough the morning with a few thunderstorms possible by noon. \nCoverage will be decreasing by mid-afternoon with drier \nconditions moving into the region through Monday. Temperatures \nwill again be warm today, but a degree or so cooler than \nyesterday with highs in the mid/upper 70s to low/mid 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A front will approach late Monday bringing more\nwidespread chances of showers and thunderstorms. However, flow\naloft will be increasingly zonal resulting in weaker surface\nfeatures and less forcing along the boundary. So, strong storms\nare not very likely. Winds behind the front will align with Lake\nChamplain supporting rough lake conditions late Monday into\nTuesday. Another dry period is likely Tuesday afternoon through\nWednesday with temperatures and RH continuing to slowly \ndecrease. Highs around 80 degrees with dew points in the 50s \nwill feel like a treat compared to recent muggy conditions. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Unsettled weather is expected to continue through the \nsecond half of next week, with chances for showers and thunderstorms \nand an overall trend towards cooler temperatures towards the \nweekend. Zonal flow across the region will allow for several quick \nshortwaves to move through, bringing chances for showers although \nthe progressive flow will limit hydro concerns. As of now, Thursday \nlooks to be the day with the greatest chances for showers, with some \npossible thunderstorms in the afternoon. Of course, the timing of \nany of these shortwaves and shower chances may chance as we get \ncloser. Looking towards the end of the week, the upper-level flow \nwill amplify as ridging builds over the western US with downstream \ntroughing over the eastern US/Canada. The cyclonic flow over the \nnortheastern US will favor a continuation of scattered showers with \nslightly below normal temperatures for the weekend.",
